</style>

<div class="wrap">

<header>
  <span class="eyebrow">Pf-PeptideFilter · Revision working note</span>
  <h1>Reviewer 2: threshold sensitivity and the vaccine-antigen benchmark</h1>
  <p class="lede">The combined R2.1 / R2.3 analysis has been run; &sect;A answers each request in turn and carries the scientific review. Four of the draft plan&rsquo;s ten sensitivity scenarios do not work &mdash; three change nothing and one empties the catalogue &mdash; and the antigen benchmark returns “excluded” in every cell. Replacements are proposed and tested. Six wording corrections are copy-ready, five already carried in this report.</p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span><b>From</b> Andrew Balmer</span>
    <span><b>Analysis</b> <code>analysis/</code> on <code>add-manuscript-and-wiki</code></span>
    <span><b>App</b> pf-peptidefilter.streamlit.app</span>
    <span><b>Verified against</b> live <code>prod</code> 5861f79, 4 Sep 2026</span>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- Plain -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">Plain</span><h2>What this analysis did, in plain terms</h2></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>What was asked, and why.</b> Reviewer 2 asked for two related checks. R2.3: is the catalogue Pf-PeptideFilter produces stable when the main thresholds move, or an artefact of one hand-picked setting? R2.1: how do well-known malaria vaccine antigens behave under the same filtering logic? Jacob&rsquo;s brief was to answer both together, since the antigen panel is most useful read alongside the threshold sensitivity rather than as a separate pass/fail exercise.</p>
    <p><b>What the app does.</b> Pf-PeptideFilter starts from predicted peptides and keeps genes only if they pass five filters: similarity to human proteins, conservation across parasite variation data, low indel burden, expression in the selected life-cycle stage, and an optional orthology requirement. The conservation filter, for instance, asks whether enough peptides from a gene are conserved at or above the selected parasite-frequency threshold &mdash; so a gene can fail because too few of its peptides are conserved, even where individual peptides look good.</p>

    <p><b>A worked example, because this one detail governs everything else.</b> Each filter is measured <i>per peptide</i>, then summarised to the gene by a <b>percentile</b> rather than by counting how many peptides passed. Take <code>PF3D7_0308800</code>, a real gene in the 253. It has ten peptides, with haplotype frequencies 0.9876, 0.9960, 0.9960, 0.9987, 0.9990, 0.9990, 0.9991, 0.9995, 0.9996 and 0.9999.</p>

    <p>The conservation threshold is 0.99, and the gene rule is set to &ldquo;95% of peptides&rdquo;.
    Count them and <b>nine of the ten pass — 90%</b>, which is short of 95%, so on a plain reading
    the gene should be dropped. The app instead takes the 5th percentile of those ten values, which
    interpolates between the lowest two and gives <b>0.9914</b>; since 0.9914 is above 0.99, the
    gene <b>passes</b>.</p>

    <p>So &ldquo;95% of peptides must pass&rdquo; describes the control&rsquo;s label rather than the arithmetic underneath. The two agree for most genes and diverge for a minority: 19 of the 253 are retained on fewer than 95% of their peptides actually meeting the threshold. That is why the methods paragraph needed rewording, why the antigen table reports the slider setting and the true peptide fraction as separate columns, and why a reader should not convert one into the other. The indel filter is the exception &mdash; its rule sits at 100%, so the percentile is the maximum and &ldquo;every peptide must pass&rdquo; is literally true.</p>

    
    <p><b>The reference catalogue.</b> The stricter M253 setting is the app default plus liver-stage day-4 expression and <i>P. vivax</i> orthology. It retains 253 genes and 10,088 peptides. The app&rsquo;s default catalogue retains 1,142 genes and 41,332 peptides, and M253 is a strict subset of that broader default output.</p>
    <p><b>The sensitivity analysis.</b> Fifteen configurations were run. Each changes one filter from the M253 reference, then records catalogue size and overlap with both M253 and the app default at gene and peptide level. Four of the ten draft scenarios were rejected for testing nothing useful &mdash; the two human-identity settings (&le;90% and &le;70%) were inert, an indel-stringent setting was inert, and requiring perfect conservation emptied the catalogue &mdash; and a fifth was replaced because its value cannot be selected in the app. Empirically meaningful, on-grid alternatives took their place, with the rejected options kept in <code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code>.</p>
    <p><b>The antigen benchmark.</b> The eight requested antigens were resolved to stable PlasmoDB gene IDs and checked in every run: CSP, AMA1, RH5, TRAP, CelTOS, EBA175, MSP1 and LSA3. &ldquo;Excluded&rdquo; means the gene did not survive all active filters for that run. It does <em>not</em> mean the antigen is biologically poor, non-immunogenic or irrelevant as a vaccine target. The antigen table reports the slider setting being tested, the underlying fraction of peptides passing, and a genome-wide percentile rank, so a near miss can be told from a severe failure.</p>
    <p><b>What was found.</b> Single-filter catalogue sizes range from 176 to 1,142 genes around the 253-gene M253 baseline. Relaxing filters adds genes, tightening removes them, and combined relaxations are super-additive because some genes fail more than one filter at once. None of the eight benchmark antigens re-enters the catalogue in any of the 15 scenarios. All eight fail the configured strain-conservation criterion, five of the eight sit in the bottom 1% of all genes on the conservation percentile, and a sixth is just outside at 2.03%. The interpretation is narrow: the tool rejects these established antigens because they fit this conservation-first logic poorly, and the analysis says nothing about their vaccine efficacy.</p>
    <p><b>What this cannot tell us.</b> The benchmark does not test immune protection, epitope presentation, formulation, clinical performance, or whether a rejected antigen is a bad target. The life-stage and orthology filters are also not equally fair to all eight, because the panel mixes pre-erythrocytic and blood-stage biology and includes <i>P. falciparum</i>-restricted targets. The conservation conclusion is the robust one: even with expression and orthology relaxed, or swapped to a sporozoite-stage setting, the eight are still not retained.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- A -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">A</span><h2>Response to each request</h2></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Everything asked for in Jacob&rsquo;s email and the attached plan, with what was produced and where it lives. Quotations are verbatim from the email and from Steps 1&ndash;20. Four requests were answered <em>differently</em> from the way they were specified; the reasons are below.</p>
    <p>Reviewer 2 asked for two things: <b>R2.1</b>, benchmark the licensed and leading malaria vaccine antigens, and <b>R2.3</b>, add a threshold sensitivity analysis. The email&rsquo;s decision was to answer them together &mdash; <em>&ldquo;The idea here is to address R2.1 and R2.3 together in a single sensitivity-analysis run, rather than treating them as two separate analyses.&rdquo;</em> &mdash; so one set of 15 configurations carries both answers.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Asked for</th><th>Delivered, and where</th><th>Status</th><th>What it showed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;one main supplementary sensitivity table covering the baseline and relaxed/stringent scenarios&rdquo;</td><td>15 rows &times; 22 columns. <code>supplementary_table_A_sensitivity.csv</code>; trimmed to 8 columns in &sect;03</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Catalogue ranges 176&ndash;1,142 genes across single-filter moves from a 253-gene baseline</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;comparison with both the default catalogue and the 253 gene catalogue&rdquo;</td><td>Shared count, % retained and Jaccard against <b>both</b> references, at gene <em>and</em> peptide level &mdash; 12 of the 22 columns</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>M253 is a strict subset of APP: 253 of 1,142 genes, Jaccard 0.222</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;antigen retention for every scenario (column)&rdquo;</td><td><code>benchmark_antigens_retained</code>, column 22 of Table A; also the full 8 &times; 15 grid in <code>antigen_retention_matrix.csv</code></td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Reads <span class="mono">none retained</span> in all 15 rows &mdash; spelled that way rather than <span class="mono">None</span>, which pandas reads back as a missing value. Dropped from the report&rsquo;s <em>display</em> table for that reason &mdash; it is in the CSV, and this is the disclosure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;one smaller antigen-focused supplementary table explaining when each antigen gene passes or fails&rdquo;</td><td>8 rows &times; 32 columns. <code>supplementary_table_B_antigens.csv</code>; 9 columns shown in &sect;04</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>All eight fail conservation; <code>M253_exclusion_reason</code> lists every filter each one fails, not just the first</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;three settings for each filter: relaxed, default and stringent, with a short rationale for each choice&rdquo;</td><td>Five filters &times; three settings, rationale per value in &sect;03</td><td><span class="chip fail">answered differently</span></td><td>The indel filter <b>has no stringent direction on any of its four controls</b> &mdash; it gets a relaxation-only pair. Verified, not assumed: see &sect;03</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;vary one filter at a time, keeping all other filters fixed&rdquo;</td><td>All 15 scenarios differ from M253 in one filter only</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Holds for all 15, though three rows change two <em>settings</em> of the human filter &mdash; see the departures below</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;two reference baselines&rdquo; &mdash; application defaults, and &ldquo;the 253 gene example, using the exact configuration that generated that result&rdquo;</td><td><code>config_APP.json</code>, <code>config_M253.json</code>, both replayable</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>APP 1,142 / 41,332; M253 253 / 10,088. The email&rsquo;s description of M253 as &ldquo;default + activating the last two filters&rdquo; is exactly right and reproduces</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;For every run, we record the number of retained genes and peptides and the overlap with both reference outputs&rdquo;</td><td>Columns 4&ndash;17 of Table A; plus per-run gene and peptide catalogues, 45 files in <code>run_outputs/</code></td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>All 15 reproduce from their frozen configs; containment verified by set inclusion at both levels</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;check if the antigens requested by the reviewer are retained or discarded: CSP, AMA1, RH5, TRAP, CelTOS, EBA175, MSP1 and LSA3&rdquo;</td><td>All eight, identified by stable PlasmoDB IDs, checked in all 15 runs</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>None retained in any of the 15. Identifiers are not substring matches &mdash; MTRAP, TRAPPC2/4/5, MSP10 and MSP11 are separate rows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;the interpretation should be that we are testing how established vaccine antigens behave under the specific filtering logic of the app, not if they are good or bad vaccine targets&rdquo; &mdash; flagged <b>Important</b></td><td>&sect;04 opening and closing paragraphs</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>&sect;04 states it explicitly and the &ldquo;working as intended&rdquo; reading is marked as interpretation rather than output</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ldquo;check the scenario definition and the rationale and more importanly if they make sense empirically (e.g., perhaps one configuration removes all genes which is obviously meaningless)&rdquo;</td><td><code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code> &mdash; five scenarios tested and discarded with reasons</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td><b>This is where the real findings came from.</b> Four of the plan&rsquo;s ten sensitivity scenarios do nothing or empty the catalogue; the exact case Jacob anticipated occurred (conservation = 1.00 &rarr; 0 genes)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 10 &mdash; the per-run output list (catalogue size; shared/%/Jaccard vs M253 and vs APP at both levels; direction-of-change counts)</td><td>All 18 specified quantities, as 22 columns with scenario/filter/setting/antigens</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Every numeric cell recomputes independently</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 12 &mdash; &ldquo;benchmark-antigen identifier table&rdquo; as a named output</td><td>Folded into Table B: <code>antigen</code>, <code>gene_id</code>, <code>gene_name_in_app</code>, <code>n_peptides</code></td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Not a separate file; the four identifier columns lead Table B instead</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 14 &mdash; &ldquo;report the relevant <b>percentage of peptides passing</b> rather than only a binary PASS/FAIL&rdquo;, to distinguish &ldquo;94% of peptides pass a 95% requirement&rdquo; from &ldquo;only 20% pass&rdquo;</td><td><code>*_true_pct_peptides_passing</code> for all three peptide-extrapolated filters, alongside <code>*_gene_rule_pct_tolerated</code></td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Reported for all three peptide-extrapolated filters. Conservation, % of peptides passing: AMA1 27.4, TRAP 22.8, CelTOS 50.0, MSP1 54.7, LSA3 69.7, EBA175 72.0, CSP 74.4, RH5 80.8, against a 95% requirement</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 14 &mdash; &ldquo;final M253 status&rdquo; and &ldquo;final APP status&rdquo; per antigen</td><td><code>M253_status</code>, <code>APP_status</code></td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>&ldquo;excluded&rdquo; in all 16 cells</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Steps 8, 9 and 15 &mdash; &ldquo;The analysis therefore requires <b>12 unique runs</b>&rdquo; / &ldquo;should have <b>12 rows</b>&rdquo;</td><td>15 runs</td><td><span class="chip fail">answered differently</span></td><td>12 + 3 identity-only rows. The count difference is entirely the human filter: see departure 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 17 / Step 20 &mdash; an 8 &times; 12 retained/excluded matrix, &ldquo;particularly well as a simple supplementary heatmap&rdquo;, to show whether an antigen &ldquo;re-enters the catalogue under a modest relaxation&rdquo;</td><td>Replaced by the distance-to-threshold figure. Matrix still shipped as <code>antigen_retention_matrix.csv</code> (8 &times; 15)</td><td><span class="chip fail">answered differently</span></td><td>Every cell is &ldquo;excluded&rdquo;, so the heatmap is one flat block and cannot show what Step 17 wanted. The figure answers the same question &mdash; how far from selectable is each antigen &mdash; which a uniform grid cannot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 19 &mdash; reproducibility outputs: a frozen config per run, gene- and peptide-level output per run, the identifier table, both supplementary tables, the retention matrix, and the scripts</td><td>15 configs, 45 run files, both tables, the matrix, the analysis/verification/rendering scripts, <code>provenance.txt</code>, <code>COLUMN-DEFINITIONS.md</code>, and the source of this report</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>All 15 configs replay from disk to their table rows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 19 / Step 2 &mdash; &ldquo;The scenario definitions should be frozen before the filtering outputs are inspected&rdquo;</td><td>Scenarios were frozen, run, and then four were replaced after inspection</td><td><span class="chip fail">answered differently</span></td><td>Directly contradicted by the email&rsquo;s instruction to check the scenarios empirically. The final 15 were <b>not</b> pre-registered: see departure 3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 20 &mdash; a short Results summary with five named components</td><td>Proposed passage at the end of this section</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Drafted here for the first time, all five components covered, clearly marked as proposed text</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 6 &mdash; &ldquo;The 5-CPM threshold is a <b>prespecified sensitivity-analysis value</b>, not a biologically validated boundary &hellip; It should be described as such&rdquo;</td><td>Stated in &sect;03 alongside the expression scenarios</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Stated explicitly wherever the 5&nbsp;CPM value is used</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Step 18 &mdash; recovery of an antigen &ldquo;provides a useful face-validity benchmark &hellip; but does not demonstrate that its retained peptides are immunogenic or protective&rdquo;; an antigen &ldquo;may fail because it is highly polymorphic, despite having demonstrated biological or immunological relevance&rdquo;</td><td>&sect;04</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Both sentences now carried explicitly; the second is the substance of the result</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The email&rsquo;s closing invitation: &ldquo;Feel free to modify the plan or suggest changes&rdquo;</td><td>Four departures, below, each with the evidence that forced it</td><td><span class="chip pass">answered</span></td><td>Three of the four were forced by the empirical check Jacob asked for</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>The four departures from the plan</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>1 &mdash; The human filter varies alignment length, not identity, and gains three identity-only rows.</b> Step 3 is explicit: <em>&ldquo;For the principal sensitivity analysis, only the <b>maximum percentage identity</b> will be varied. Alignment length, the Joint rule and the gene-level peptide requirement will remain fixed at their M253 values&rdquo;</em>, and under &ldquo;Important design choice&rdquo;: <em>&ldquo;Do <b>not</b> simultaneously change identity, alignment length and gene-peptide proportion. Doing so would make it impossible to determine which component of the human-similarity filter caused the observed change.&rdquo;</em></p>

    <p>The delivered analysis does both prohibited things, because the scenarios as specified measure nothing. With everything else held at M253, the identity threshold returns <b>exactly 253 genes at &le;60%, &le;70%, &le;80%, &le;90% and &le;100%</b>: the &le;15&nbsp;aa alignment-length criterion binds across that whole range, so identity never gets to act. Varying length instead gives 183 / 253 / 376 genes at &le;12 / &le;15 / &le;18&nbsp;aa.</p>

    <p>On why &plusmn;3&nbsp;aa, which the plan specified for identity but not for length: it is the smallest symmetric step that moves the catalogue materially in both directions (&minus;28% and +49%) while leaving the criterion recognisable. At &le;20&nbsp;aa, the top of the slider, the filter excludes nothing; at &plusmn;1&nbsp;aa too few genes move to separate a real dependence from granularity in the alignment lengths. Like the plan&rsquo;s own &plusmn;10 points on identity, it is a defensible span rather than a validated boundary. Switching the length filter off, which the app allows, makes identity informative again: 506 / 558 / 587 genes at &le;70% / &le;80% / &le;90%. Separating the arms meets Step 3&rsquo;s attribution concern by construction, since each row moves one component. Largest departure here, and the first to raise with Jacob.</p>
    <p><b>2 &mdash; The indel filter has no stringent arm.</b> All four of its controls were checked. The gene rule already sits at its 100% maximum. The frequency threshold is inert from 0.05 down to 0.00, because every gene at or below 0.05 sits at exactly 0.0 and the next distinct value in the data is 0.057. And restricting <em>which</em> indels are counted <b>relaxes</b> the filter, since the metric is a maximum over a smaller set: &ldquo;Only Insertions&rdquo; gives 280 genes, &ldquo;Only Deletions&rdquo; 260, &ldquo;Only Frameshifts&rdquo; 294, insertions+frameshifts 297, against 253 at baseline. Relaxation-only is a property of the filter at this configuration, and belongs in the Results as a finding.</p>
    <p><b>3 &mdash; The final fifteen scenarios were not pre-registered.</b> Steps 2 and 19 both require the definitions to be fixed before anyone looks at the outputs. The email asks for the opposite: <em>&ldquo;check the scenario definition and the rationale and more importanly if they make sense empirically (e.g., perhaps one configuration removes all genes which is obviously meaningless)&rdquo;</em>. Vetting requires looking, so both cannot be honoured. What happened: the plan&rsquo;s scenarios were defined, run, and four replaced once their outputs were seen &mdash; three for being inert, one for emptying the catalogue. That is what the email asked for, but it makes the analysis exploratory at that step, and it should not be written up as pre-registered. <code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code> records all five rejected or superseded options with reasons.</p>
    <p><b>4 &mdash; The conservation-stringent arm moved from a threshold to a gene rule.</b> The plan specified 1.00, which empties the catalogue: only 19 of 4,937 genes reach it, and none survives the other filters. 0.995 works and gives 164 genes, but the app&rsquo;s haplotype-frequency slider steps in units of 0.01, so 0.995 cannot be selected and the next value above 0.99 is 1.00. The arm tightens the gene rule instead &mdash; <code>haplotype_gene_pc = 99</code>, 176 genes, on-grid, and the same control the preprint already moves to get its published 1,517 figure.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">A defect in a shipped column &mdash; <code>num_peptides</code> is short by one</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The <code>num_peptides</code> column in the application&rsquo;s own <code>gene-metrics-filtering.csv.gz</code> is <b>exactly one less</b> than the gene&rsquo;s true peptide count for <b>4,423 of 4,937 genes</b>; the difference is &minus;1 or 0 and never anything else. Summed over the whole table it gives 374,902 against 379,325 actual peptide rows, and over the published catalogue <b>9,861 against the correct 10,088</b>.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">No published figure is affected, because every peptide count in the paper and in these outputs comes from counting peptide rows rather than summing this column, and <code>validate()</code> confirms 10,088 independently. The column is copied verbatim into all 15 per-run gene catalogues in <code>run_outputs/</code>, though, so a reviewer who sums it lands 227 peptides away from the paper. Drop it from those files or recompute it before <code>analysis/</code> is deposited anywhere citable. The defect is in the upstream data file and worth reporting to the app&rsquo;s maintainers separately.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Scientific review</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The checks above establish that the code does what it says. What follows asks whether the <em>science</em> holds &mdash; whether the comparisons are fair and the inferences warranted. Eight concerns, with what each one changes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">S1 &middot; Life-stage mismatch in the antigen panel</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The eight antigens are not all from one parasite stage. CSP, TRAP, CelTOS and LSA3 are pre-erythrocytic; AMA1, MSP1, RH5 and EBA175 are blood-stage. M253 requires <b>liver-stage day-4 expression</b>, and six of the eight fail that filter &mdash; so on the face of it the benchmark asks merozoite antigens to be expressed in liver stage, which is close to a category error.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Two things complicate that reading. Failure does not track stage: CSP (pre-erythrocytic) and AMA1 (blood-stage) <em>both pass</em> the day-4 filter, while LSA3, a liver-stage antigen, fails it at &ge;1&nbsp;CPM in only 1 of 3 replicates (11.04, 0, 0). And the headline does not rest on those filters at all. Swapping to the app’s <b>sporozoite</b>-stage filter, the stage-appropriate choice for three of the four pre-erythrocytic antigens, gives 695 genes and <b>still retains none of the eight</b>. Removing expression and orthology <em>entirely</em> gives 1,142 genes and still retains none. <b>All eight fail strain conservation</b>, which is stage-independent and applies to them equally.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">So the conclusion is robust, but the per-filter exclusion reasons need the caveat. Seven of the eight pass sporozoite expression at &ge;1&nbsp;CPM in all five replicates (EBA175 is the exception, at 2 of 5), so the expression failures are largely a stage artefact. Orthology is similar: RH5 is <i>P. falciparum</i>-restricted with no clear <i>P. vivax</i> orthologue. <b>Conservation is the only filter that <em>all eight</em> fail</b> &mdash; but other stage-independent filters do discriminate. Drop conservation, keep human similarity and indels, and 2,397 genes return, of which only <b>three of the eight re-enter</b> (AMA1, RH5, CelTOS). Human similarity independently excludes CSP; indels exclude TRAP, EBA175, MSP1 and LSA3, MSP1&rsquo;s maximum indel frequency being 0.817 against a 0.05 threshold. Three of the five filters therefore discriminate on stage-independent grounds. The write-up should say that rather than presenting five independent failures.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">S2 &middot; Is the benchmark circular?</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The tool selects for conserved targets, and these eight antigens are famous for antigenic variation &mdash; the paper&rsquo;s own Discussion says so. A benchmark that rejects them therefore risks confirming only that the filter does what it is defined to do. As a pass/fail table, that objection largely stands.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Expressing the same data as a <b>percentile rank against every other gene</b> defeats it, because the result becomes graded and falsifiable rather than binary. (4,930 genes have a conservation value, 4,933 an identity value and all 4,937 an indel value; the missing ones lack the metric.) On conservation the eight are extreme outliers: AMA1 and CelTOS beat <b>0.0%</b> of the genome, TRAP 0.1%, CSP 0.3%, MSP1 0.8%, EBA175 2.0%, LSA3 12.6%, RH5 17.5%. <b>Five of the eight sit in the bottom 1% of all genes &mdash; AMA1 0.02%, CelTOS 0.04%, TRAP 0.14%, CSP 0.30%, MSP1 0.83% &mdash; with EBA175 sixth at 2.03%.</b> They could have come out mid-pack and did not.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">The contrast is what makes it informative, and it needs the scales stated, because the three are not comparable. Ties at the best value cap what any gene can score: the conservation percentile reaches <b>99.6%</b>, but human identity tops out at <b>56.7%</b> (2,138 genes tie there) and indel frequency at <b>30.0%</b> (3,457 tie). The eight span 6.9% to 56.7% on identity, six of them at or near that ceiling, and five sit exactly at the indel ceiling of 30.0%, with MSP1 at 1.9% and LSA3 at 7.4%. On identity and indels most of these antigens are about as good as a gene can be; only on conservation are they extreme. The claim is therefore specific &mdash; genomic outliers <em>on conservation in particular</em>, ordinary elsewhere &mdash; which is a real result rather than a restatement of the filter&rsquo;s definition. The columns are in Table B (<code>*_genome_percentile</code>) and they are the number to quote in the Results.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">S3 &middot; One filter at a time understates sensitivity by about 2.5&times;</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The email scoped this deliberately: <em>&ldquo;This is by no means an exhaustive analysis but we are not going to iterate through hundreds of combinations&rdquo;</em>. The design is a decision, and a factorial sweep is not the remedy. But the report should not leave a reader assuming the design explores the space, because it demonstrably cannot.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Interactions exist and are measurable. The clearest is already in &sect;03: identity is inert only because the alignment-length criterion binds. Another: tightening conservation to the 99% gene rule costs <b>77 genes</b> at the default human setting but <b>119</b> when the human gene rule is loosened to 50%, so the cost of one filter depends on another. Some pairs do not interact at all &mdash; relaxing orthology and relaxing the indel gene rule add <em>disjoint</em> gene sets and combine exactly additively (328 = 253 + 37 + 38).</p>
    <p style="margin:0">In aggregate the compounding is large. Relaxing all five filters at once gives <b>2,048 genes</b> against an additive prediction of 958 from the five individual arms, which gain 705 between them — a gain of 1,795 where one-at-a-time predicts 705. That is the combination using the indel <em>gene-rule</em> arm (<code>alignment_length&nbsp;&le;18</code>, <code>strain_conservation&nbsp;&ge;0.95</code>, <code>indel_gene_pc&nbsp;=&nbsp;95</code>, &ge;2 D4 replicates, no orthology); substituting the indel <em>frequency</em> arm (<code>&le;0.10</code>) gives <b>1,565 genes</b> against a prediction of 927. Both are exploratory cross-checks run outside Table A and recorded in <code>analysis/output/interaction_checks.txt</code>. This is what conjunctive filters do &mdash; relaxing one admits only genes already passing the other four &mdash; and it means the single-filter table bounds the catalogue&rsquo;s sensitivity from below rather than estimating it. <b>One option:</b> an extra row for the all-relaxed configuration would bound the space for the cost of one run. Beyond what was asked for, so Jacob&rsquo;s call.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">S4 &middot; Conservation is measured in African isolates only</span>
    <p style="margin:0">Strain conservation comes from haplotype frequencies in <b>8,492 African isolates</b>, a subset of Pf7&rsquo;s 16,203 genomes. The paper states the scope in Methods, in Table 1 and in the Results, and the plan&rsquo;s Step 4 confirms that the 1.00 setting &ldquo;requires the selected haplotype to be observed in all callable <b>African</b> samples&rdquo;. What neither the paper nor this report does is flag it as a <em>limitation</em>.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">It is one. Conservation measured in one continental population says nothing about conservation elsewhere, so a catalogue built on African frequencies inherits that scope and should be described as an African-frequency catalogue. This is a limitation of the <em>data</em>; it is not a claim about any antigen&rsquo;s geographic allele-frequency structure, which would need a citation this analysis does not supply. For a vaccine-target shortlist it is material and belongs in the Discussion in one sentence.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">The sensitivity analysis cannot address it. The application ships a single African-derived conservation metric, so there is no second geography to vary, and no setting of any filter can test geographic robustness with these data.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">S5 &middot; The 0.99 threshold cannot be checked against sampling error</span>
    <p style="margin:0">Each peptide&rsquo;s haplotype frequency is an estimate from a finite sample, so a hard cut at 0.99 will move genes for statistical as well as biological reasons. Whether that matters cannot be assessed from the shipped data: <b>neither the gene nor the peptide table carries a coverage or callable-sample count</b>, so there is no denominator. Nor are the frequencies integer multiples of 1/8,492 &mdash; only 14% are consistent with that, and the observed granularity is ~1.4&times;10&#8315;&#8312; &mdash; so the per-peptide sample size cannot be recovered and no binomial interval can be constructed. That limit belongs in the write-up rather than being glossed.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">What can be measured is how fragile the catalogue is to the threshold itself. Of the 253 retained genes, <b>18 have a 5th-percentile haplotype frequency inside [0.990, 0.991)</b>, within 0.001 of the cut; 33 fall within 0.002 and 89 within 0.005. The closest sits at 0.99007, seven parts in 100,000 above the line. Genome-wide, 1,218 genes fall in [0.985, 0.995). A sub-1% movement in the threshold therefore reshuffles a material fraction of the catalogue, which is exactly where sampling noise would live. Read it as a caveat on the precision of &ldquo;253&rdquo;.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">S6 &middot; &ldquo;Day 2 is unusable&rdquo; is a library-depth artefact</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The day-2/4/5/6 comparison earlier in this report is correct as counts, but the inference drawn from it was wrong. There is no library-size column; the totals are diagnostic anyway. Summed CPM per replicate: <b>day 2 &mdash; 679,169 / 433,162 / 15,682</b>; day 4 &mdash; 755,198 / 1,012,068 / 537,141; day 5 &mdash; 913,866 / 1,019,839 / 974,892; day 6 &mdash; 834,453 / 801,665 / 949,551; sporozoite &mdash; 1.8 to 5.9 million.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">CPM summed over a transcriptome should be 10&#8310;. It is, at days 5 and 6. It is not at day 2, and one day-2 replicate totals <b>15,682 &mdash; 1.6% of a day-5 replicate</b>. These are parasite CPM within libraries dominated by host hepatocyte reads, so at day 2 there is almost no parasite material to measure: 3,367 of 4,937 genes are zero in all three day-2 replicates, against 46 at day 5 and 8 at day 6. Since the rule requires all three replicates, the day-2 figure of 36 genes is effectively set by that one near-empty replicate.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">That still supports day 4, though for a different reason than the one given: day 2 cannot support a replicate-based expression filter <em>in these libraries</em>, which is a property of parasite biomass in the source experiment. It also means <b>&ldquo;&ge;1 CPM&rdquo; is not a constant stringency across stages</b> &mdash; the totals differ by up to ~380&times; &mdash; so cross-stage readings of the expression filter, and the &ge;5&nbsp;CPM stringent arm, are depth-relative. M253 uses day 4 alone, so the published result is internally consistent; the caveat applies to the cross-stage comparison.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout note">
    <span class="eyebrow">S7 &middot; Statistics and reporting</span>
    <p style="margin:0">No inferential statistics are used anywhere, which is correct for a descriptive sensitivity analysis. Checked: nothing in the report claims anything that would need a test, a p-value or a confidence interval.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Every scenario is nested with respect to M253 by construction, verified by set inclusion at gene and peptide level for all 15. Containment is more interpretable than Jaccard for nested sets, and Table A reports both (<code>M253_genes_pct_retained</code>, <code>APP_genes_pct_retained</code>). <b>The trap:</b> M253&rsquo;s Jaccard against APP is 0.222, which invites the reading that the two catalogues disagree. They do not &mdash; M253 is a <em>perfect subset</em> of APP and 100% of its genes are retained there. The 0.222 is entirely the 889-gene size difference. Read containment whenever the sets are nested and differently sized.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">The replacement scenarios were chosen after seeing the originals fail, which is legitimate exploratory work. It is disclosed in <code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code> and in departure 3 above, and the final fifteen are nowhere presented as prespecified.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout note">
    <span class="eyebrow">S8 &middot; Two peptide definitions</span>
    <p style="margin:0">Three numbers exist for the same runs and all three are legitimate: 10,088 peptides <em>belonging to</em> the 253 retained genes; 84,859 for the independently filtered peptide set at the same configuration; and 326,701 for <em>both</em> conservation-only rows, because peptide-level filtering strips the percentile suffix and so cannot see the gene rule at all. Table A&rsquo;s <code>peptides</code> column uses the belonging definition throughout, <code>run_outputs/</code> ships both per run, and <code>validate()</code> gates 84,556 and 42,208 on the belonging definition, which is what the paper reports. The recommendation is simple: state the definition once in Methods and use it everywhere.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Proposed Results summary (Step 20)</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Step 20 asks for &ldquo;a short summary&rdquo; covering five named components. This is a <b>proposed passage for the Results</b> &mdash; it is not in the manuscript and no manuscript file has been edited. Every figure comes from the outputs in <code>analysis/output/</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="draft">
    To assess how sensitive the illustrative catalogue is to the chosen thresholds, we varied each of the five filters in turn while holding the other four at the published configuration. <span class="fix">This gave thirteen single-filter variants, which together with the two reference configurations make fifteen runs in total (Supplementary Table A). Four of the five filters were varied in both directions; the indel filter admits relaxation only, because the published configuration already requires every peptide in a gene to pass and the frequency threshold is inert at every value below 5%.</span> Candidate-set size ranged from 176 to 1,142 genes against a baseline of 253. <span class="fix">Composition was nested in the direction each variant moves: every relaxation retained all 253 published genes, and each of the three configurations that tightened a criterion was a strict subset of them.</span> The catalogue was most sensitive to the expression and conservation criteria &mdash; relaxing the day-4 replicate requirement from three replicates to two more than doubled it to 596 genes (gene-set Jaccard 0.42 against the published catalogue), and lowering the haplotype-frequency threshold from 0.99 to 0.95 added 164 genes &mdash; and least sensitive to the indel frequency threshold, which added 7 genes (Jaccard 0.97) across its full usable range. The published catalogue is a strict subset of the one produced by the application&rsquo;s default settings: all 253 genes are retained among the 1,142 obtained with the two optional filters inactive, the difference being entirely the addition of the liver-stage expression and <i>P. vivax</i> orthology requirements.

    None of the eight benchmark vaccine antigens was retained under the published configuration, nor under any of the fifteen runs examined (Supplementary Table B). Strain conservation was the criterion responsible in every case: all eight fell below the requirement that a gene&rsquo;s 5th-percentile peptide haplotype frequency reach 0.99 &mdash; the percentile form of the application&rsquo;s &ldquo;95% of peptides&rdquo; gene rule &mdash; with the proportion of qualifying peptides ranging from 22.8% (TRAP) to 80.8% (RH5), and five of the eight ranking within the bottom 1% of all genes on this metric. <span class="fix">Conservation was not, however, the only criterion to exclude these antigens on grounds independent of life stage: applying the human-similarity and indel filters alone returns 2,397 genes and restores only three of the eight, with circumsporozoite protein excluded by human similarity and TRAP, EBA175, MSP1 and LSA3 by indel frequency.</span> <span class="fix">Six of the eight did not meet the liver-stage day-4 expression requirement, which for the four blood-stage antigens in the panel reflects the life stage assayed rather than a property of the gene; seven of the eight are expressed in sporozoites, and substituting the sporozoite-stage criterion returned 695 genes without restoring any antigen to the catalogue.</span>

    These results indicate that the catalogue&rsquo;s size is materially dependent on the expression and conservation thresholds while its composition is stable under relaxation, and that established vaccine antigens are excluded by this workflow principally because <span class="fix">they are among the most polymorphic genes in the 8,492 African isolates from which the conservation metric is derived</span> &mdash; a consequence of the conservation-first design rather than an assessment of their merit as vaccine targets. <span class="fix">Because conservation is estimated from African genomes only, the catalogue should be understood as conserved with respect to that population rather than globally.</span>
  </div>
  <p class="note-under">That covers all five of Step 20&rsquo;s components in order: degree of change in counts and composition; which filters produced the greatest sensitivity; similarity between the M253 and application-default catalogues; which benchmark antigens were retained under M253 (none, stated directly); and which filters excluded the rest. The stage caveat from S1, the percentile ranks from S2 and the African-only scope from S4 are folded in, since each changes what the passage is entitled to claim &mdash; it no longer blames the non-conservation failures on life stage alone, four indel failures and one human-similarity failure being stage-independent, and it no longer calls the identity-only rows tightenings, since they gain genes.</p>
</section>

<!-- 1 -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">01</span><h2>How the numbers were produced</h2></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Everything below is a run of the published app's own filtering engine — <code>filter_datasets()</code> in <code>pep/filter.py</code> — against the repository's own <code>config/filters.json</code> and <code>data/*.csv.gz</code>. No filter condition has been reimplemented anywhere in the analysis.</p>
    <p>The app collects widget values into <code>st.session_state</code> and hands them to that one function, which touches Streamlit in exactly two lines (both plain reads of the settings). Substituting a dictionary for those reads lets the same code be driven from a script, which is what makes the 269 configurations this analysis runs feasible where clicking sliders is not. (Counted by instrumenting <code>run()</code>, not estimated: 24 in <code>run_sensitivity.py</code> &mdash; four validation runs, fifteen scenarios, five rejected &mdash; and 245 in <code>run_antigens.py</code>, most of them threshold and gene-rule sweeps.)</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Provenance checks against the live repository</h3>
  <p class="note-under" style="margin-bottom:-4px">Checked against <code>github.com/malariagen/pf-peptidefilter</code> fetched 4 September 2026 — <code>prod</code> at <code>5861f79</code>, <code>main</code> at <code>3a9af72</code> — not against a cached copy. Every app file in the working tree used for the analysis is byte-identical to live <code>main</code>.</p>
  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Live <code>prod</code> (5861f79)</th><th>What was run</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="name">gene-metrics-filtering.csv.gz</td><td class="mono">md5 fdee05f9…</td><td><span class="chip ok">identical</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">peptide-metrics-filtering.csv.gz</td><td class="mono">md5 3380de0f…</td><td><span class="chip ok">identical</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">go-data-core.csv</td><td class="mono">md5 compared</td><td><span class="chip ok">identical</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">config/filters.json</td><td class="mono">byte-for-byte</td><td><span class="chip ok">identical</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">UI defaults (all thresholds)</td><td class="mono">UI_CONFIG</td><td><span class="chip ok">identical</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">Filtering logic</td><td class="mono">peptidefilter/filter.py</td><td>differs only in import order and one summary column renamed <code>proportion</code> → <code>fraction</code>; no difference in filtering behaviour</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">Environment</td><td class="mono">pandas 1.5.1</td><td><span class="chip ok">pinned to match</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Reproduction of published figures</h3>
  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Result in the preprint</th><th class="num">Published</th><th class="num">Reproduced</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Conservation alone, 95% of peptides at ≥99%</td><td class="num">2,107 genes</td><td class="num">2,107</td><td><span class="chip ok">match</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Conservation at 99% of peptides</td><td class="num">1,517 genes</td><td class="num">1,517</td><td><span class="chip ok">match</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Conservation + human identity</td><td class="num">1,274 genes</td><td class="num">1,274</td><td><span class="chip ok">match</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>All five filters (the illustrative catalogue)</td><td class="num">253 / 10,088</td><td class="num">253 / 10,088</td><td><span class="chip ok">match</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note-under">These four are run as an assertion at the start of every analysis script (<code>pepfilter_headless.validate()</code>); the analysis aborts if any of them drifts.</p>
</section>

<!-- 2 -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">02</span><h2>Corrections to the new methods paragraph</h2></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The preprint’s <em>default-thresholds</em> sentence reproduces the result correctly. It describes the catalogue as <em>"default thresholds (human identity &le; 80%, strain conservation &ge; 99%, indel frequency &lt; 5<mark style="background:var(--reject-soft);color:var(--reject)">.</mark> homology with <i>P. vivax</i>, gene expression in day 4 of liver stage infection)"</i></em> — and it reproduces 253 genes and 10,088 peptides exactly. Two details in that sentence and its neighbour still need correcting, though neither changes a number: the operator is <code>&le;</code> rather than <code>&lt;</code>, and the preceding sentence names both <i>P. vivax</i> and <i>P. berghei</i> where the run used <i>P. vivax</i> alone. Both are in the “two smaller mismatches” callout below.</p>
    <p>But the two sentences immediately before it, describing how the 2,107 and 1,274 figures were obtained, do <b>not</b> match the configuration that produces them, and unlike the methods paragraph they are already in print. Every <em>count</em> in the preprint reproduces; it is the prose describing how the counts were reached that is wrong.</p>
    <p>The expanded paragraph drafted for the revision spells those settings out in more detail. Three of its clauses do not describe what the code does. The first is a wrong number; the other two describe the gene-level rule in a way the implementation does not support.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>1 — the indel gene rule was 100%, not 95%</h3>
  <blockquote class="wrong">"For indel filtering, <span class="hl">at least 95% of peptides</span> within each gene were required to pass the ≤5% frequency threshold, considering indels of any type or predicted effect."</blockquote>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">What it actually was</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The indel gene rule was left at the application default of <b>100% of peptides</b>. At 95% the catalogue is <b>291 genes / 13,772 peptides</b>, not 253 / 10,088. The other two figures in that paragraph — 75% for human identity, 95% for conservation — are correct.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>2 — "applied jointly" describes something the app does not do</h3>
  <blockquote class="wrong">"For human identity filtering, <span class="hl">at least 75% of peptides within each gene were required to pass, with the identity and alignment-length criteria applied jointly</span> and a maximum alignment length of 15 amino acids."</blockquote>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">What it actually was</span>
    <p style="margin:0">Two <em>separate</em> percentile columns are tested and combined with <code>AND</code>: <code>blt_pident_p75 &le; 80</code> and <code>blt_length_p75 &le; 15</code>. Each is the 75th percentile of that one metric across the gene's peptides, computed independently of the other. The app never asks whether the <em>same</em> 75% of peptides satisfies both conditions.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">The distinction changes the catalogue. <b>10 of the 253 retained genes</b> have fewer than 75% of their peptides satisfying both conditions at once, so a filter written the way the paragraph describes would drop them — <code>PF3D7_1203200</code> (70.0% of peptides pass both), <code>PF3D7_0721100</code> (70.8%), <code>PF3D7_0628500</code> (71.0%), <code>PF3D7_0722700</code> (71.4%), <code>PF3D7_1237700</code> (71.4%), <code>PF3D7_1404400</code> (72.2%), <code>PF3D7_0512000</code> (72.7%), <code>PF3D7_1308100</code> (73.3%), <code>PF3D7_1015100</code> (74.4%), <code>PF3D7_1343400</code> (74.5%). Across all 4,937 genes the identity-passing and length-passing peptide subsets differ for <b>4,846</b> of them. (4,840 is the count whose passing <em>fractions</em> differ; a further 6 genes pass equal numbers of peptides on each criterion but not the same ones — exactly the case the word “jointly” would have to cover.)</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Separately: the <code>human_id_joint_rule</code> setting itself has <b>no effect on any result</b>. Applying two column tests in one nested group or in two sequential steps is the same set intersection, and a run of both options returns byte-identical gene sets (253 under M253, 1,142 under application defaults). The radio button changes only how the app groups the two tests in its own step summary — one row rather than two.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>3 — the conservation clause has the same problem</h3>
  <blockquote class="wrong">"For strain conservation, <span class="hl">at least 95% of peptides within each gene were required to meet the &ge;99% frequency threshold</span>, using the dominant amino-acid haplotype irrespective of whether it matched the 3D7 reference."</blockquote>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">What it actually was</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The code tests <code>hap_top_hap_freq_p05 &ge; 0.99</code>. The suffix is <code>_p{100 &minus; haplotype_gene_pc}</code>, an inversion the human and indel filters do not have — and the direction is right: requiring the 5th percentile to clear 0.99 is the percentile form of "95% of peptides must pass". But it is a percentile, interpolated linearly between the two peptides that straddle it, not a count. <b>19 of the 253 retained genes</b> have fewer than 95% of their peptides at &ge;0.99 and are kept anyway; <code>PF3D7_0308800</code> is retained on 90.0% (10 peptides, 9 passing, 5th percentile 0.9914).</p>
    <p style="margin:0">The indel clause needs no such repair. Its rule is <code>_p100</code>, the maximum, where the percentile and the count agree exactly — "all peptides within each gene" is literally true, on all 4,937 genes.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Verified against the four published figures, which still reproduce exactly: only <code>_p05</code> gives 2,107 genes (<code>_p04</code> gives 1,913, <code>_p06</code> gives 2,291), so the inversion is both implemented and used correctly.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>A last point worth making explicit while we are in there: <b>10,088</b> is the number of peptides <em>belonging to</em> the 253 retained genes, as reported on the app's gene-results page. Pf-PeptideFilter also filters peptides independently, and that count for the same configuration is <b>84,859</b>. Both are legitimate; the paper should say which one it means.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Errors already in print</h3>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">The 1,274 sentence — wrong by a factor of eight</span>
    <blockquote class="wrong" style="margin-bottom:6px">"Applying both the strain conservation and human identity filters simultaneously–i.e. by requiring <span class="hl">95% of peptides per gene</span> to exceed a 99% conservation threshold <span class="hl">and</span> remain below 80% identity to any human exon with a maximum alignment length of 15 amino acids–reduced the number of retained genes to 1,274"</blockquote>
    <p style="margin:0">The human gene rule is <b>75%</b>, not 95%; the sentence attaches 95% to both filters. Run as described it gives <b>158 genes</b>, not 1,274. And "simultaneously … and" invites the joint-subset reading, which gives <b>92</b>: of the app’s 1,274, only 92 have ≥95% of peptides passing conservation and human identity together. The 1,274 comes from conservation’s 5th-percentile test at ≥0.99 <em>and</em> identity’s 75th-percentile test at ≤80% <em>and</em> length’s 75th-percentile test at ≤15 aa, each evaluated separately — 1,274 / 158 / 92 for the three readings.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">The 2,107 sentence — the parenthetical names the wrong threshold</span>
    <blockquote class="wrong" style="margin-bottom:6px">"… retain only those genes for which at least 95% of peptides exhibited a dominant haplotype (i.e. a haplotype present in <span class="hl">95% of isolates or more</span>), a total of 2,107 genes and 84,556 peptides were retained"</blockquote>
    <p style="margin:0">It should read <b>99%</b>. The parenthetical conflates the gene rule (95% of peptides) with the frequency threshold (0.99). At a 0.95 threshold the same gene rule returns <b>3,824 genes</b>; 2,107 requires 0.99. The counts themselves are right — 2,107 / <b>84,556</b> and 1,517 / <b>42,208</b> all reproduce exactly, and both peptide figures are now asserted by <code>validate()</code> alongside the other four. Note the independently filtered peptide set is <b>326,701 for both</b> rows, because peptide-level filtering strips the percentile suffix and so cannot see the gene rule at all: further confirmation that every peptide number in the paper is the "belonging to the retained genes" definition.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">Two smaller mismatches in the same passage</span>
    <p style="margin:0"><b>"indel frequency &lt; 5%"</b> — the code tests <code>≤ 0.05</code>. Here it changes nothing: every gene at or below 0.05 sits at exactly 0.0 and the next distinct value is 0.057, so strict and non-strict both give 3,457 genes. Worth aligning anyway, since a reviewer reproducing the work will notice.</p>
    <p style="margin:0"><b>"cross-species homology (presence of one-to-one orthologs in <i>P. vivax</i>, and <i>P. berghei</i>)"</b> — the published configuration requires <i>P. vivax</i> only. Requiring both gives <b>251 genes</b>, not 253. The very next sentence says "homology with <i>P. vivax</i>", so the two disagree with each other.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Redrafted paragraph</h3>
  <div class="draft">
    As an illustrative analysis, we applied the application's default thresholds for human identity (≤80%), strain conservation (≥99%) and indel frequency (≤5%), together with two filters that are not active by default: liver-stage expression and cross-species homology. All remaining settings were left at their defaults. <span class="fix">For human identity filtering, genes were retained where the 75th percentile of peptide identity was ≤80% and the 75th percentile of peptide alignment length was ≤15 amino acids, each percentile evaluated separately across the gene’s peptides; the two criteria therefore apply to the same gene but need not be met by the same subset of its peptides.</span> <span class="fix">For strain conservation, genes were retained where the 5th percentile of peptide haplotype frequency was ≥99% — the percentile form of the application’s “at least 95% of peptides” gene rule — using the dominant amino-acid haplotype irrespective of whether it matched the 3D7 reference.</span> For indel filtering, <span class="fix">all peptides within each gene (100%)</span> were required to pass the ≤5% frequency threshold, considering indels of any type or predicted effect. For the expression filter, genes were required to have expression of ≥1 CPM in all three biological replicates at liver-stage day 4. Day 4 was selected as a representative intermediate liver-stage time point because it provides substantially greater parasite transcript coverage than day 2, while preceding the late liver-stage transcription observed at days 5 and 6 <span class="todo">[ref still needed]</span>.<span class="fix"> [This is now supported by the application’s own expression table, which is worth citing alongside any external reference — see the note below.]</span> For the homology filter, we required the presence of a <i>P. vivax</i> orthologue, as <i>P. vivax</i> is a major human malaria parasite and therefore provides a biologically relevant cross-species comparator, while also illustrating the functionality of the homology filter. Under this configuration, <span class="fix">253 genes were retained, collectively containing 10,088 peptides. Because Pf-PeptideFilter applies gene-level and peptide-level filtering independently, we report here the total number of peptides belonging to the retained genes.</span> This represents a shortlist of potential vaccine targets for downstream experimental validation rather than a uniquely optimal candidate set.
  </div>

  <div class="callout note">
    <span class="eyebrow">The Day-4 claim is supported by the app’s own data</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The <code>[ref still needed]</code> marker is still a blocker for an external citation, but the claim is no longer resting on one. Counting genes at ≥1 CPM in all three replicates in <code>data/gene-metrics-filtering.csv.gz</code>:</p>
    <div class="tablebox" style="margin:4px 0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Liver stage</th><th class="num">Genes ≥1 CPM in all 3 replicates</th><th class="num">Genes ≥1 CPM in ≥1 replicate</th><th class="num">Median CPM</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="name">Day 2</td><td class="num">36</td><td class="num">1,486</td><td class="num">0.00</td></tr>
          <tr class="ref"><td class="name">Day 4</td><td class="num">1,583</td><td class="num">4,364</td><td class="num">22.09</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="name">Day 5</td><td class="num">4,375</td><td class="num">4,766</td><td class="num">64.77</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="name">Day 6</td><td class="num">4,657</td><td class="num">4,793</td><td class="num">67.45</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin:0">Day 2 is unusable at this rule — 36 genes of 4,937, median CPM 0.00 — while days 5 and 6 are close to saturation (4,375 and 4,657, i.e. 89% and 94% of all genes), at which point the filter stops discriminating. Day 4 at 1,583 genes is the only one of the four that is both well-powered and selective, which is precisely the "representative intermediate" argument, from the data the app ships. Cite the table; add the external reference when Jacob supplies it.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Completeness check on the redraft</h3>
  <p class="note-under" style="margin-bottom:2px">All 17 settings in the frozen configuration
  (<code>analysis/output/configs/config_M253.json</code>) are accounted for: 16 are stated in the
  paragraph above, and the 17th — <code>human_id_joint_rule</code> — is deliberately not, because
  it does not change the output. Nothing else is left implicit. Checked setting by setting against
  the JSON.</p>
  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th class="mono">Value</th><th>Where the paragraph states it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="name">human_id_gene_rule</td><td class="mono">min % of peptides</td><td>"the 75th percentile of peptide identity … and the 75th percentile of peptide alignment length"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">human_id_gene_pc</td><td class="mono">75</td><td>"the 75th percentile" — same clause</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">human_id_joint_rule</td><td class="mono">Joint</td><td><b>not stated, by design</b> — the setting has no effect on the gene or peptide set (verified: both options return the identical 253 genes). It changes only the app’s step summary, so stating it in Methods would imply a constraint that is not applied.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">identity_percent</td><td class="mono">80</td><td>"human identity (≤80%)"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">alignment_length</td><td class="mono">15</td><td>"maximum alignment length of 15 amino acids"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_rule</td><td class="mono">min % of peptides</td><td>"the 5th percentile of peptide haplotype frequency … the percentile form of the application’s ‘at least 95% of peptides’ gene rule"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc</td><td class="mono">95</td><td>"at least 95% of peptides" — same clause, via the <code>100−pc</code> inversion</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">haplotype_type</td><td class="mono">Any haplotype</td><td>"irrespective of whether it matched the 3D7 reference"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">strain_conservation</td><td class="mono">0.99</td><td>"the 5th percentile … was ≥99%"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">indel_frequency</td><td class="mono">0.05</td><td>"the ≤5% frequency threshold"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">indel_gene_pc</td><td class="mono">100</td><td>"all peptides within each gene (100%)" — the corrected clause</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">indel_type</td><td class="mono">Any</td><td>"indels of any type"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">indel_frameshfits</td><td class="mono">Any</td><td>"or predicted effect"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">expression_rule</td><td class="mono">All replicates</td><td>"in all three biological replicates"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">cpm_cutoff</td><td class="mono">1</td><td>"expression of ≥1 CPM"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">expression_stage</td><td class="mono">Liver Stage Day 4</td><td>"at liver-stage day 4"</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">homology_species</td><td class="mono">P. vivax</td><td>"the presence of a <i>P. vivax</i> orthologue"</td></tr>
