  </div>
</section>

<!-- 3 -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">03</span><h2>R2.3 — Threshold sensitivity</h2></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>One <em>filter</em> is varied at a time; every other filter stays at the published configuration. That is not always one <em>setting</em>: the three identity-only rows change the human filter’s combination mode as well as its threshold (<code>human_id_joint_rule</code> → “deactivate length filter”, plus <code>identity_percent</code> for the ≤90% and ≤70% rows; the ≤80% row changes the mode alone). It is unavoidable, since identity is inert while the length criterion is active, and it is the point of those rows. Peptide counts are peptides belonging to the retained genes, on the paper’s definition. Jaccard is on gene sets &mdash; overlap divided by union, so 1.0 means identical catalogues and 0 no shared gene. It falls when two catalogues differ greatly in <em>size</em> even where one wholly contains the other, so for the nested comparisons here the &ldquo;% retained&rdquo; columns are more informative.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr>
        <th>Scenario</th><th>Filter</th><th>Exact setting</th>
        <th class="num">Genes</th><th class="num">Peptides</th>
        <th class="num">Jaccard vs published</th>
        <th class="num">Gained</th><th class="num">Lost</th>
      </tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="ref"><td class="name">APP</td><td>—</td><td>application defaults</td><td class="num">1,142</td><td class="num">41,332</td><td class="num">0.222</td><td class="num">889</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr class="ref"><td class="name">M253</td><td>—</td><td>published baseline</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">10,088</td><td class="num">1.000</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">HUMAN-RELAXED</td><td>human</td><td>alignment length ≤18 aa</td><td class="num">376</td><td class="num">14,735</td><td class="num">0.673</td><td class="num">123</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">HUMAN-STRINGENT</td><td>human</td><td>alignment length ≤12 aa</td><td class="num">183</td><td class="num">7,261</td><td class="num">0.723</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">70</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">HUMAN-IDONLY-RELAXED</td><td>human</td><td>identity ≤90%, length filter off</td><td class="num">587</td><td class="num">21,554</td><td class="num">0.431</td><td class="num">334</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">HUMAN-IDONLY-BASELINE</td><td>human</td><td>identity ≤80%, length filter off</td><td class="num">558</td><td class="num">20,706</td><td class="num">0.453</td><td class="num">305</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">HUMAN-IDONLY-STRINGENT</td><td>human</td><td>identity ≤70%, length filter off</td><td class="num">506</td><td class="num">19,125</td><td class="num">0.500</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">CONSERVATION-RELAXED</td><td>conservation</td><td>haplotype frequency ≥0.95</td><td class="num">417</td><td class="num">23,144</td><td class="num">0.607</td><td class="num">164</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">CONSERVATION-STRINGENT</td><td>conservation</td><td>99% of peptides at ≥0.99 (gene rule)</td><td class="num">176</td><td class="num">5,007</td><td class="num">0.696</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">77</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">INDEL-RELAXED-FREQ</td><td>indel</td><td>indel frequency ≤0.10</td><td class="num">260</td><td class="num">10,708</td><td class="num">0.973</td><td class="num">7</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">INDEL-RELAXED-RULE</td><td>indel</td><td>95% of peptides must pass</td><td class="num">291</td><td class="num">13,772</td><td class="num">0.869</td><td class="num">38</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">EXPRESSION-RELAXED</td><td>expression</td><td>≥1 CPM in ≥2 replicates (D4)</td><td class="num">596</td><td class="num">23,321</td><td class="num">0.424</td><td class="num">343</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">EXPRESSION-STRINGENT</td><td>expression</td><td>≥5 CPM in all replicates (D4)<span class="mk" style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400"> †</span></td><td class="num">251</td><td class="num">10,031</td><td class="num">0.992</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">ORTHOLOGY-RELAXED</td><td>orthology</td><td>no orthology requirement</td><td class="num">290</td><td class="num">11,085</td><td class="num">0.872</td><td class="num">37</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">ORTHOLOGY-STRINGENT</td><td>orthology</td><td><i>P. vivax</i> AND <i>P. berghei</i></td><td class="num">251</td><td class="num">10,024</td><td class="num">0.992</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note-under">† The 5-CPM value is a <b>prespecified sensitivity-analysis setting, not a biologically validated boundary</b> between expressed and non-expressed genes, and should be described that way wherever it appears (plan Step 6). CPM here is parasite-relative within libraries dominated by host reads, so its stringency is not constant across stages — see S6 in §A.</p>
  <p class="note-under">Consistency holds throughout, checked by set containment: M253 ⊆ every relaxed catalogue, and every tightened catalogue ⊆ M253, at gene and peptide level across all 15 runs. One label needs care — <span class="mono">HUMAN-IDONLY-STRINGENT</span> is named for its identity threshold (≤70%, the most stringent of that arm) but is a <em>relaxation</em> overall, because switching the length filter off removes a constraint; it gains 253 genes, loses none, and is a superset. No benchmark antigen is retained under any scenario, so that column is omitted here — see §04. Full overlap statistics against both reference catalogues are in <code>supplementary_table_A_sensitivity.csv</code>.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>What this says</h3>
    <p>The catalogue is <b>most sensitive to expression and conservation</b>: relaxing the Day-4 replicate rule more than doubles it (596 genes, Jaccard 0.42), and dropping conservation to 0.95 adds 164 genes. For <b>indels and orthology</b> it depends which axis moves. The indel <em>frequency</em> threshold barely matters (+7 genes, +2.8%, Jaccard 0.97) and tightening orthology costs 2 genes, but relaxing the indel <em>gene rule</em> to 95% adds 38 (+15.0%, Jaccard 0.87) and dropping orthology altogether adds 37 (+14.6%, Jaccard 0.87). Calling those two “a few percent” would understate them. Composition is stable in the sense that matters: no reasonable setting of any single filter removes a substantial part of the published list except by deliberate tightening.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Scenarios from the draft plan that had to be replaced</h3>
  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>As planned</th><th class="num">Genes</th><th class="num">Δ</th><th>Why it was replaced</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="dead"><td class="name">Human relaxed — identity ≤90%</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">0</td><td>No effect. Under the joint rule the ≤15 aa length criterion is binding at every identity setting from 60% upward, so both planned values land on the baseline. Identity only becomes limiting below 60% (252 genes at ≤59%, 232 at ≤50%).</td></tr>
        <tr class="dead"><td class="name">Human stringent — identity ≤70%</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">0</td><td>No effect, same reason.</td></tr>
        <tr class="dead"><td class="name">Indel stringent — ≤0.01</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">0</td><td>No effect. The gene-level indel metric is a step function: identical for any cutoff from 0.00 to 0.05.</td></tr>
        <tr class="dead"><td class="name">Conservation stringent — =1.00</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">−253</td><td>Degenerate. Only 19 of 4,937 genes reach it; none survive the other filters.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout note">
    <span class="eyebrow">Worth one sentence in the Results</span>
    <p style="margin:0">That the human identity threshold is inert over its upper range while the alignment-length criterion is active is a genuine property of the filter. It is inert from ≤60% to ≤100%, the whole span the plan proposed to vary; below 60% it does bite (252 genes at ≤59%, 232 at ≤50%). Holding identity at ≤80% and varying length instead gives 183 / 253 / 376 genes at ≤12 / ≤15 / ≤18 aa. Switching the length filter off — an option the app already offers — makes identity meaningful again: 506 / 558 / 587 genes at ≤70% / ≤80% / ≤90%.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>For indels there is no stringent arm, checked on every axis the filter has. The frequency threshold is inert all the way down: 0.05, 0.04, 0.03, 0.02, 0.01 and 0.00 all return the same 253 genes, because every gene at or below 0.05 sits at exactly 0.0. The gene rule is already at its 100% maximum. And restricting <em>which</em> indels are counted <b>relaxes</b> the filter — “Only Insertions” 280 genes, “Only Deletions” 260, “Only Frameshifts” 294, insertions-and-frameshifts 297 — since counting fewer indels lowers each gene’s maximum. The filter is <b>maximally stringent at its default settings</b>, with no stringent direction available anywhere.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">One caveat on the conservation-stringent arm</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The stringent conservation arm tightens the <b>gene rule</b>, not the threshold. The app’s haplotype-frequency control is a slider with <code>min 0.0, max 1.0, step 0.01</code>, so the only selectable values are 0.00, 0.01 … 1.00: <b>0.995 is not one of them</b>, and neither is 0.999. There is no on-grid threshold alternative either, because the next selectable step above 0.99 is 1.00, which empties the catalogue. Earlier drafts used <code>strain_conservation = 0.995</code> (164 genes), a real run that could not have been reproduced by clicking the published app; it has been replaced. Holding the threshold at the published 0.99 and moving the gene rule instead keeps every scenario reproducible in the app — here is that axis in the full M253 configuration:</p>
    <div class="tablebox" style="margin:4px 0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th class="mono">Column</th><th class="num">Genes</th><th class="num">Peptides</th><th>On the app’s grid?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc = 95</td><td class="mono">_p05</td><td class="num">253</td><td class="num">10,088</td><td><span class="chip ok">baseline</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc = 96</td><td class="mono">_p04</td><td class="num">228</td><td class="num">8,700</td><td><span class="chip pass">yes</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc = 97</td><td class="mono">_p03</td><td class="num">206</td><td class="num">6,994</td><td><span class="chip pass">yes</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc = 98</td><td class="mono">_p02</td><td class="num">186</td><td class="num">5,582</td><td><span class="chip pass">yes</span></td></tr>
          <tr class="ref"><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc = 99</td><td class="mono">_p01</td><td class="num">176</td><td class="num">5,007</td><td><span class="chip ok">adopted</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="name">haplotype_gene_pc = 100</td><td class="mono">_p00</td><td class="num">170</td><td class="num">4,791</td><td><span class="chip pass">yes</span></td></tr>
          <tr class="dead"><td class="name">strain_conservation = 0.995 <i>(superseded)</i></td><td class="mono">_p05</td><td class="num">164</td><td class="num">5,171</td><td><span class="chip fail">no — off-grid</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin:0">The adopted arm is <b><code>haplotype_gene_pc = 99</code> — 176 genes / 5,007 peptides</b>, a strict subset of the published catalogue (0 gained, 77 lost, Jaccard 0.696). What decides it is that the preprint <em>already</em> reports a 99% conservation gene rule — it is how the published 1,517-gene figure is obtained — so the Methods can cite a value the analysis already uses, and anyone clicking the published app can reproduce the scenario. Table A, the retention matrix, the 15 frozen configs and <code>run_outputs/</code> have been regenerated; the 0.995 run is kept in <code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code> with its reason.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 4 -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">04</span><h2>R2.1 — The vaccine-antigen benchmark</h2></div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>All eight antigens are excluded, under every one of the fifteen configurations.</b> The retained/excluded matrix the plan proposed is therefore a single uniform block, and the "antigens retained" column reads <span class="mono">none retained</span> in every row. Neither is publishable as designed — but the reason behind them is a strong result.</p>
    <p>The Discussion already makes this argument in words:</p>
  </div>

  <blockquote>"Previous vaccine efforts have repeatedly encountered issues with limited efficacy due to antigenic variation among parasites, including trials targeting antigens such as circumsporozoite protein (CSP), apical membrane antigen 1 (AMA1), or merozoite surface protein 1 (MSP1). These vaccines have often resulted in strain-specific immune responses…"</blockquote>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The benchmark supplies the numbers behind that sentence. All eight fail on <b>strain conservation</b>, and AMA1 and MSP1 &mdash; two of the three the paper names &mdash; fail by a wide margin on both measures (27.4% and 54.7% of peptides against a 95% requirement; 0.20 and 0.49 against 0.99). CSP needs stating carefully: it misses badly on the threshold it tolerates (0.34 against 0.99) but is among the closest of the eight on the peptide-fraction axis (<b>74.4%</b> of its peptides clear 0.99, second only to RH5&rsquo;s 80.8%; the 70 in the table is the gene-rule slider setting, not a peptide fraction). The two axes rank the eight differently, so neither should be quoted alone.</p>

    <p>So the answer to &ldquo;does your tool recover the known antigens?&rdquo; is no &mdash; and it rejects them for exactly the reason the paper already identifies as having limited those vaccines in the field. That second half is an <em>interpretation</em> consistent with the computation, though the computation does not establish it. What the computation establishes is narrower: all eight fail the configured conservation criterion, and conservation is the filter responsible in every case. The step to &ldquo;working as intended&rdquo; rests on the paper&rsquo;s own argument about antigenic variation. Framed that way it is still the stronger answer to R2.1, and it claims no more than was shown.</p>
    <p>Nothing here says whether these are good or bad vaccine targets. It says how established antigens behave under the criteria Pf-PeptideFilter implements. Step 18 asks for two things to be stated explicitly, and both hold: an established antigen <em>&ldquo;may fail because it is highly polymorphic, despite having demonstrated biological or immunological relevance&rdquo;</em> &mdash; which is what the percentile ranks in S1&ndash;S2 show for all eight &mdash; and, conversely, recovery would have <em>&ldquo;provide[d] a useful face-validity benchmark for the workflow but does not demonstrate that its retained peptides are immunogenic or protective&rdquo;</em>. Nothing here speaks to immunogenicity or protection in either direction.</p>
    <p>One point of precision for the response, since the reviewer asked about <em>&ldquo;licensed and leading&rdquo;</em> antigens and the two are not the same. Of the eight, only <b>CSP</b> underlies a licensed product &mdash; both WHO-recommended and prequalified malaria vaccines, RTS,S/AS01 and R21/Matrix-M, are CSP-based. The other seven are clinical or preclinical candidates. Describe the panel as the eight the reviewer requested, <em>one licensed and seven leading candidates</em>, and read nothing here as evaluating a licensed vaccine.</p>
    <p>One caveat on the per-filter failures below, established in S1 of &sect;A: <b>conservation</b> is the only filter all eight fail, though human similarity independently excludes CSP and indels independently exclude four more. Six of the eight fail the day-4 expression filter, but seven are expressed in sporozoites and substituting the sporozoite criterion retains none of them; RH5 lacking a <i>P. vivax</i> orthologue is expected biology for a <i>P. falciparum</i>-restricted gene. Count the conservation column, and treat expression and orthology as largely stage-explained.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
