        <tr><td class="name">AMA1</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_1133400</td><td class="num">88<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">25<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.20<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">100<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">3<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">yes<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td>conservation</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">RH5</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_0424100</td><td class="num">85<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">80<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.93<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">100<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">2<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">no<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td>conservation, expression, orthology</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">TRAP</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_1335900</td><td class="num">75<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">22<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.26<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">93<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">2<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">yes<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td>conservation, indel, expression</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">CelTOS</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_1216600</td><td class="num">88<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">40<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.22<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">100<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">2<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">yes<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td>conservation, expression</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">EBA175</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_0731500</td><td class="num">85<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">70<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.58<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">98<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">no<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td>conservation, indel, expression, orthology</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">MSP1</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_0930300</td><td class="num">85<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">50<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.49<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">92<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">2<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">yes<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td>conservation, indel, expression</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">LSA3</td><td class="mono">PF3D7_0220000</td><td class="num">85<span class="mk p">✓</span></td><td class="num">60<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">0.89<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">95<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">1<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td class="num">no<span class="mk f">✗</span></td><td>conservation, indel, expression, orthology</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="tablebox">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Antigen</th><th class="num">% of peptides passing conservation<br>(requirement 95%)</th><th class="num">Gene-rule slider setting<br>tolerated</th><th class="num">Conservation percentile<br>among all 4,937 genes</th><th class="num">Identity percentile</th><th class="num">Indel percentile</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="name">AMA1</td><td class="num">27.4</td><td class="num">25</td><td class="num">0.0</td><td class="num">45.0</td><td class="num">30.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">TRAP</td><td class="num">22.8</td><td class="num">22</td><td class="num">0.1</td><td class="num">16.7</td><td class="num">16.6</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">CelTOS</td><td class="num">50.0</td><td class="num">40</td><td class="num">0.0</td><td class="num">51.5</td><td class="num">30.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">CSP</td><td class="num">74.4</td><td class="num">70</td><td class="num">0.3</td><td class="num">6.9</td><td class="num">30.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">MSP1</td><td class="num">54.7</td><td class="num">50</td><td class="num">0.8</td><td class="num">56.7</td><td class="num">1.9</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">EBA175</td><td class="num">72.0</td><td class="num">70</td><td class="num">2.0</td><td class="num">50.2</td><td class="num">19.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">LSA3</td><td class="num">69.7</td><td class="num">60</td><td class="num">12.6</td><td class="num">56.7</td><td class="num">7.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="name">RH5</td><td class="num">80.8</td><td class="num">80</td><td class="num">17.5</td><td class="num">56.4</td><td class="num">30.0</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note-under">The two quantities the main table cannot carry, both in <code>supplementary_table_B_antigens.csv</code>. <b>Column 2</b> is what plan Step 14 asked for &mdash; the actual proportion of each gene&rsquo;s peptides satisfying the peptide-level condition, which distinguishes &ldquo;94% of peptides pass a 95% requirement&rdquo; from &ldquo;only 20% pass&rdquo;. It is <em>not</em> column 3, the slider setting; the two differ in 4 of the 24 antigen &times; filter cells. <b>Columns 4&ndash;6</b> rank each gene against all 4,937, read as &ldquo;better than X% of the genome&rdquo;, so a low number is a bad gene. They are what makes the benchmark more than a restatement of the filter&rsquo;s definition: five of the eight are in the bottom 1% of the genome for conservation while sitting mid-range on identity. Sorted by conservation percentile, worst first.</p>

  <p class="note-under">✓ passes that filter, ✗ fails it. Every verdict is a run of the app with all filters neutralised except the one under test. Each percentage is read off the app&rsquo;s own gene-filtering slider &mdash; the highest &ldquo;% of peptides that must pass&rdquo; setting at which the gene survives &mdash; so the values step through the 39 settings the slider offers rather than being computed independently. Each was checked to be the correct floor on that grid, and the pass/fail sequence is monotone in the setting for all eight genes on all three filters. Read them as slider settings, <em>not</em> as the exact share of peptides passing. </p>

  <p class="note-under">Because the gene rule tests a linearly interpolated percentile, the setting can sit either side of the grid floor of the true peptide fraction, and does for <b>4 of these 24 cells</b>: CelTOS conservation reads 40 where 50.00% of its peptides are at &ge;0.99 (grid floor 50); EBA175 human reads 85 where 83.33% pass both conditions (floor 82); TRAP indel reads 93 where 92.98% pass (floor 92); MSP1 indel reads 92 where 91.86% pass (floor 91). No verdict changes, but the numbers should not be quoted as percentages of peptides. </p>

  <p class="note-under">&ldquo;Max freq&rdquo; is the most stringent haplotype-frequency threshold the gene still meets at the 95%-of-peptides rule, found by sweeping the slider at its own 0.01 step. The CSV columns holding these values are <code>*_gene_rule_pct_tolerated</code>; they were previously <code>*_pct_peptides_passing</code>, which asserted exactly the wrong thing. Full definitions for both tables are in <code>analysis/output/COLUMN-DEFINITIONS.md</code>.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Four dot-plot panels showing, for each of eight antigens, the highest gene-rule setting it still satisfies for conservation, human similarity and indels, and the highest haplotype frequency it tolerates, each against the requirement. All eight fall short on strain conservation.">
    <figcaption><b>Proposed supplementary figure.</b> Distance to each threshold, replacing the retained/excluded matrix. It separates AMA1 — which fails conservation by a wide margin on both measures, 25% of peptides and a tolerated threshold of 0.20 — from RH5 and LSA3, which come far closer on the threshold axis (0.93 and 0.89 against a 0.99 requirement). Note that the two panels rank the eight differently: LSA3 is nearly at the requirement in panel B but further from it than CSP in panel A. A uniform grid of "excluded" cannot show that. Colour carries no meaning, so the figure survives greyscale printing and colour blindness; a hollow marker fails the filter, a filled one passes.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- 5 -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">05</span><h2>Proposed outputs</h2></div>
  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>Supplementary Table A</dt><dd>The 15 configurations above — sizes, overlap with both reference catalogues, antigen retention. <code>supplementary_table_A_sensitivity.csv</code></dd>
    <dt>Supplementary Table B</dt><dd>The eight antigens, their per-filter values and PASS/FAIL calls, and the filters responsible for exclusion. <code>supplementary_table_B_antigens.csv</code></dd>
    <dt>Supplementary Figure</dt><dd>Distance to threshold, as above. <code>supplementary_figure_antigen_thresholds.pdf</code></dd>
    <dt>Dropped</dt><dd>The 8 × 12 retained/excluded matrix the plan proposed — the artefact actually generated, <code>antigen_retention_matrix.csv</code>, is 8 × 15, the three extra human arms having been added after the plan was written. Uniformly "excluded", so it carries no information. The CSV is kept for completeness.</dd>
    <dt>Also kept</dt><dd>A short table of the five scenarios tested and rejected or replaced, so the choices remain traceable. <code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code></dd>
    <dt>Per-run archive</dt><dd>The frozen settings for each of the 15 runs (<code>configs/</code>, 15 JSON files) and the gene-level and peptide-level catalogue for each (<code>run_outputs/</code>, 45 files).</dd>
    <dt>Provenance record</dt><dd>Re-runnable check of code and data against the live repository. <code>analysis/verify_provenance.sh</code> → <code>provenance.txt</code></dd>
    <dt>This document</dt><dd><code>analysis/report.html</code>, rendered to PDF by <code>analysis/make_report_pdf.py</code>. Keep the two together: the PDF cannot be regenerated without the source. The renderer is pinned in <code>analysis/requirements-report.txt</code> (WeasyPrint 70.0) and deliberately kept out of the repository’s <code>requirements.txt</code>, which is the app’s dependency set and is byte-compared against live <code>main</code>. Pagination is version-dependent, so page numbers should not be cited without the pin and a fresh <code>pdfinfo</code> check.</dd>
  </dl>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="eyebrow">What "reproducible" does and does not cover</span>
    <p style="margin:0">The <em>app and data</em> the analysis ran against are verified byte-identical to live <code>main</code> (§01). The <em>analysis itself</em> is not in the repository — <code>analysis/</code> is untracked, so a third party cloning <code>malariagen/pf-peptidefilter</code> gets the app and the data but not these scripts or outputs. That is deliberate for now, but it means the reproducibility claim is "re-runnable by us against a verified app", not "re-runnable by a reviewer from the public repo". If the revision cites this analysis, the scripts need to land somewhere citable.</p>
    <p style="margin:0">Two smaller limits. The eight antigen identifiers were checked against the <code>gene_name</code> column of the app's own gene table, which is where the analysis resolves them, rather than against a live PlasmoDB query, which needs an API key. And the deployed Streamlit instance exposes no commit metadata, so the comparison is against live <code>prod</code> at <code>5861f79</code>, not whatever the public URL is currently serving.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 6 -->
<section>
  <div class="shead"><span class="snum">06</span><h2>Open questions</h2></div>
  <div class="decision">
    <ol>
      <li><b>The human filter has two defensible sensitivity arms and I have run both</b> — varying alignment length, and varying identity with the length filter switched off. <b>Recommendation: keep both.</b> Five rows rather than two, but together they establish that identity is non-limiting under the default rule, which is worth a sentence in the Results. Drop the identity-only arm if the table needs to be tighter.</li>
      <li><b>The indel filter gets a relaxation-only pair</b>, with a sentence explaining that no stringent setting exists. <b>Recommendation: keep it that way.</b> Presenting a "stringent" setting that silently equals the baseline would be worse than stating the limit.</li>
      <li><b>Conservation stringent — now <code>haplotype_gene_pc = 99</code>, <span class="chip ok">adopted</span></b> and regenerated throughout. <b>176 genes / 5,007 peptides</b>, a strict subset of the published catalogue (0 gained, 77 lost, Jaccard 0.696), replacing <code>strain_conservation = 0.995</code> (164 genes), which the app’s 0.01 slider cannot select. The reasoning and the full gene-rule axis are in §03.</li>
      <li><b>Six wording corrections are needed, and five of them are already in print.</b> The methods paragraph needs three (the indel percentage, the "applied jointly" clause, the conservation clause — the last two change what the sentence claims the filter <em>does</em>, not just a number). The published Results need two more (the 1,274 and 2,107 sentences) plus the <code>&lt;5%</code> operator and the <i>P. berghei</i> phrasing, and the published Methods need "averaged across genes" replaced. All six are copy-ready in <code>analysis/MANUSCRIPT-CORRECTIONS.md</code> — sentence as published, replacement beneath, numbers behind each. That file is what to work from; §02 has the reasoning.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>One further wording point in the published Methods, and it is the same class of error as §02. The Methods say human identity is <em>"assessed per peptide and averaged across genes"</em>, and Table 1 says <em>"filtering is performed at the peptide level and summarised per gene"</em>. Neither is a mean: the default rule is a 75th-percentile test. "Averaged" is wrong and "summarised" is too vague to reproduce from — both should name the percentile.</p>
    <p>And a documentation bug in the deployed repository, which is worth reporting to whoever owns it. The README <b>on <code>prod</code></b> — the branch the Streamlit app is deployed from — says at line 17: <em>"Please use <code>requirements.txt</code> and Python 3.12"</em>. But <code>requirements.txt</code> pins <code>pandas==1.5.1</code>, which publishes no cp312 wheel (PyPI has cp38, cp39, cp310 and cp311 only), so it cannot be installed on the Python the README asks for without building from source. Anyone following the README's own instructions will fail at <code>pip install</code>. Either the pin or the instruction needs to move; 3.11 works today. Verified against a fresh clone of <code>prod</code> at <code>5861f79</code>.</p>
    <p class="note-under">The branch matters and is easy to get wrong: <code>main</code>’s <code>README.md</code> is a four-line stub that mentions no Python version at all, and the working tree used for this analysis matches <code>main</code>. An intermediate draft of this note checked the working tree, found nothing, and retracted the finding as unfounded — wrongly. It is recorded here rather than quietly restored.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>
  Scripts: <code>analysis/pepfilter_headless.py</code> (app driver and validation), <code>analysis/run_sensitivity.py</code>, <code>analysis/run_antigens.py</code>, <code>analysis/make_figure.py</code>, <code>analysis/run_interaction_checks.py</code>, <code>analysis/verify_conservation_grid.py</code>, <code>analysis/verify_provenance.sh</code>, and <code>analysis/make_report_pdf.py</code>. Outputs in <code>analysis/output/</code>. Scenario definitions were frozen before any output was inspected, and four were then <em>replaced</em> after their outputs were seen — three because they changed nothing and one because it emptied the catalogue. The final fifteen are therefore <b>not</b> pre-registered, which is what the email asked for (“check … if they make sense empirically”) and a departure from the plan’s Step 19. All five rejected or superseded options, with reasons, are in <code>rejected_scenarios.csv</code>. See departure 3 in §A.
</footer>

</div>
